Scroll vocabulary — clean-room

use-scroll-progress, scroll-progress, reveal-on-scroll, stagger-reveal,
scroll-snap-rail, sticky-section-list, parallax-layers,
horizontal-scroll-rail, use-min-width, pinned-steps, scroll-linked, and
text-reveal (scroll trigger) implement common scroll behaviors: a 0–1 reading
position, a viewport entrance, staggered children, CSS scroll-snap, sticky
group headers, transform-only parallax, a horizontal rail driven by vertical
scroll, a sticky scrollytelling column, and a scroll-linked fade, rise, scale,
or rotate. The ten names (scroll-triggered, scroll-linked, parallax, sticky,
pin, scroll snap, horizontal scroll, stagger, text reveal, progress bar) are
industry vocabulary. A public note from @Voxyz_ai listed those ten terms
together, and an earlier tip from @gabriell_lab suggested the first six. No
frames, wireframes, copy, or assets from those posts were copied. Motion UI
and Motion+ are not included. Supported browsers drive the reading bar with
animation-timeline scroll(root) and scaleX, parallax with a view() timeline,
and the scroll-linked presets with view() or scroll(). The hook remains the
fallback and, for the bar, the source of aria-valuenow. Reduced motion shows
entrances and text reveals immediately, holds parallax still, stacks pinned
steps, shows scroll-linked effects in their finished state, and uses a native
horizontal scroller. The reading bar still reports scroll position. Sticky
headers and scroll-snap alignment are CSS, not tweens.

